--- slope-from-points · seed 1 · difficulty 3
prompt   Find the slope between these points.
display  coordinate-plane x -6..6/1 y -6..6/1 points [(−5, 4), (−3, −2)] lines [] "Coordinate plane, x-axis −6 to 6 by 1; y-axis −6 to 6 by 1; points (−5, 4) and (−3, −2)" [slope-from-points]
answer   exact -3/1
input    keypad
keypad   allowFraction=true allowNegative=true
hint     Keep the point order consistent and divide rise by run.
step 1   Subtract y-values in one point order.   [−2 − 4 = −6]
step 2   Subtract x-values in that order too.   [−3 − −5 = 2]
step 3   Divide rise by run and reduce.   [−6/2 = −3]
miss     inconsistent-subtraction-order = 3   Subtract both coordinate pairs in the same point order.
miss     run-over-rise = -0.3333333333333333   Slope is vertical change over horizontal change: rise over run.

--- linear-vs-nonlinear · seed 12345 · difficulty 3
prompt   Is this relation linear or nonlinear?
display  coordinate-plane x -6..6/1 y -6..6/1 points [(−5, −4), (−3, −2), (−2, −1), (0, 1)] lines [] "Coordinate plane, x-axis −6 to 6 by 1; y-axis −6 to 6 by 1; points (−5, −4), (−3, −2), (−2, −1), and (0, 1)" [linear-vs-nonlinear]
answer   choice linear
input    choice
hint     Compare the exact rate of change between consecutive points.
choice   nonlinear = Nonlinear
choice   linear = Linear
step 1   Order the points by x-value.   [(−5, −4), (−3, −2), (−2, −1), (0, 1)]
step 2   Compare each consecutive slope.   [Every slope matches.]
step 3   Choose the matching classification.   [Linear]

